  transcript rather than a state; and it's real machinery for an app whose current job is showing ten checkboxes. It
  also tends to leak into the UI — filtering and sorting over a derived projection is more code than over an array.
  [3mComplexity:[0m high.

  [1mC. Mutable records only, changes diffed in memory.[0m One array of tasks. The store compares before/after on each write
  and emits change events to in-memory subscribers, persisting nothing but current state.
  [3mBuys:[0m by far the least code. You'd have a working app fastest.
  [3mCosts:[0m changes are ephemeral. A notification you didn't see because the tab was closed never existed. That's tolerable
  for solo reminders and fatal the moment a second person can change your task — which is the direction you've said
  you're heading. Retrofitting durability here means adding the log anyway, after the write paths have already spread.
  [3mComplexity:[0m trivial.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m C is cheaper today but spends its savings against exactly the future you picked — "multi-user
  shaped" and "no durable record of changes" are in direct conflict, and the write paths are the expensive thing to go
  back and centralize. B is the principled version of A, and I'd pick it for a system with real auditing requirements;
  here it buys correctness you aren't short of and charges you replay logic, snapshots, and a projection layer to get
  it. A puts the one constraint that matters — every mutation goes through a single funnel that records what changed —
  in place from the first commit, and leaves the rest ordinary.

  The honest caveat on A: the single-funnel discipline is only enforced by convention in vanilla JS. I'd make the store
  module the only thing that touches persistence and have it return frozen copies, so accidental direct mutation is at
  least noisy.
